@@ -0,0 +1,119 @@
+# Phase 1 runbook: from a built repo to the Phase 2 start line
+
+Phase 1 is finished when the **gate passes on a real model**: the reference-precision
+agent solves 5-hop HorizonGym tasks (11 dependent actions) reliably, every bit-width
+loads and runs, the lowest bit-width shows a measurable hazard, the KV cache is
+append-only, and the Phase 2 atlas fits your compute budget.
+
+Order: Mac first (free, fast iteration), then one Colab session to confirm the 8B
+headline model. Commit the gate reports and tag the repo before starting Phase 2.
+
+## 0. Sanity check (any machine, ~2 minutes)
+
+```bash
+pip install -e ".[mlx,dev]"          # Mac; on Colab: ".[cuda,dev]"
+pytest -q                            # HF backend tests skip if torch is not installed
+hazprec demo --out runs/demo         # simulated pipeline; compare with docs/demo_output.txt
+```
+
+## 1. Mac: Qwen3-1.7B (development model)
+
+```bash
+# 1a. Every bit-width converts, loads and plays one 1-hop episode (first run converts: ~5-10 min)
+hazprec smoke -c configs/mac_pilot.yaml --hops 1
+
+# 1b. Watch one episode turn by turn
+hazprec play -c configs/mac_pilot.yaml --policy uniform:8 -v
+
+# 1c. Quick gate: 10 episodes at 1 hop, 8 and 3 bits (~5 min)
+python scripts/phase1_gate.py -c configs/mac_pilot.yaml --quick
+
+# 1d. Full gate: 30 episodes at 1 and 5 hops, 8 and 3 bits (~30-60 min)
+mkdir -p reports
+caffeinate -i python scripts/phase1_gate.py -c configs/mac_pilot.yaml \
+    --out reports/gate_mac_qwen3-1.7b.json | tee reports/gate_mac_qwen3-1.7b.txt
+```
+
+`caffeinate -i` keeps the Mac awake for the run. Keep Activity Monitor's memory
+pressure in the green; four copies of a 1.7B model use about 5 GB.
+
+### Reading the gate
+
+| Check | Passes when | If it fails |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| format | ≥ 98% of turns at 8 bits parse as a tool call | `--set agent.think=false`; look at `play -v` output; try another model |
+| reliable | 8-bit success at 5 hops ≥ 0.80 | go to step 2 (3B); or `--set env.n_fields=1`, `--set env.filler_lines=2` |
+| signal | 3-bit per-turn hazard CI sits above the 8-bit CI | add a 2-bit level: `--set "backend.levels=[2,3,4,6,8]"`; or `--set env.n_fields=3` |
+| cache | no full re-render after turn 0; ≤ 48 template tokens around each observation | send me the output; the chat template needs a special case |
+| cost | the atlas in the config fits `--atlas-budget-hours` (24 h) | lower `atlas.n_tasks`, `n_samples`, `max_turns`, or drop a bit-width |
+
+Any config value can be changed on the command line with `--set`, and the same
+`--set` values must then be used for `hazprec atlas`.
+
+**What "good" looks like.** All five checks pass. The 3-bit failures should be mostly
+`wrong:ack`, `wrong:seq`, `wrong:record_id` or `wrong:count`, which are content errors
+the atlas can attribute to a phase. Many `unparseable` failures at 3 bits are a format
+collapse instead; still a valid hazard, but note it.
+
+## 2. Mac: Qwen2.5-3B (only if 1.7B fails `reliable`)
+
+```bash
+hazprec smoke -c configs/mac_pilot_3b.yaml --hops 1
+caffeinate -i python scripts/phase1_gate.py -c configs/mac_pilot_3b.yaml \
+    --out reports/gate_mac_qwen2.5-3b.json | tee reports/gate_mac_qwen2.5-3b.txt
+```
+
+Four copies of a 3B model use about 8-9 GB. Close browsers and other large apps.
+
+## 3. Colab: Qwen3-8B headline model (one session, A100 80 GB or H100)
+
+Open `colab/hazprec_colab.ipynb`, run the setup cells, then in place of the atlas cell:
+
+```bash
+!hazprec smoke -c configs/colab_8b.yaml --hops 1
+!python scripts/phase1_gate.py -c configs/colab_8b.yaml --n 20 \
+    --out $RUNS/gate_colab_qwen3-8b.json | tee $RUNS/gate_colab_qwen3-8b.txt
+```
+
+Expect `cost` to **fail** with the default config: a batch-size-1 atlas on an 8B model
+is hundreds of GPU-hours. That is the input for Phase 2 sizing, not a blocker. Every
+other check must pass. Copy the two report files into the repo's `reports/` folder.
+
+## 4. Close Phase 1
+
+```bash
+git add reports/ && git commit -m "Phase 1 gate reports"
+git tag phase1-complete
+```
+
+## 5. Phase 2 start line (weeks 3-5: sensitivity atlas, answers E1 and E2)
+
+**5a. Mac pilot atlas (start right away; runs overnight).** Use the model that passed
+the gate:
+
+```bash
+caffeinate -i hazprec atlas -c configs/mac_pilot.yaml --out runs/atlas_mac_qwen3-1.7b.jsonl
+hazprec fit --atlas runs/atlas_mac_qwen3-1.7b.jsonl --out reports/atlas_mac_qwen3-1.7b.fit.json
+```
+
+The atlas resumes if interrupted: rerun the same command. In the fit, look at:
+
+1. Per-phase `s`: are `act` and `stop` well above `read` and `think`? (first look at E1)
+2. `log2 slope` per phase: near −2 supports the 2^(−2b) model; much shallower means
+   the allocator should use fitted curves (`theory.curve_mode=empirical`).
+3. CI widths: they set how many tasks and samples the Colab atlas needs.
+
+**5b. Phase 2 engineering (built next, before the Colab atlas):**
+
+1. Batched replay sampling: the `n_samples` regenerations of a turn share one prefix
+   and run as one batch. This is what makes the 8B atlas affordable.
+2. Teacher-forced KL and entropy per segment, logged in the atlas: the data for E2
+   (do perplexity-style signals track where agents actually fail?).
+3. An E1/E2 report: share of hazard variance explained by phase, and the rank
+   correlation between entropy/KL and measured hazard, with bootstrap CIs.
+
+**5c. Colab headline atlas** once 5b lands, sized from the gate's throughput numbers.
+
+Do not pre-register the headline predictions from the Mac pilot. A pilot
+pre-registration and horizon sweep are fine as a dry run, under a `pilot_` file name;
+pre-registration files are write-once.
